14 Performance Notes
********************

14.1 Subdirectory Insertion
===========================

When working with large directory trees:

   • Use ‘dired-image-thumbnail-insert-image-subdirs’ (‘C-t s’) instead
     of ‘dired-image-thumbnail-insert-subdir-recursive’ (‘C-t z’)
   • The "smart" insertion only processes subdirectories containing
     images, which is much faster
   • For very large collections, manually insert specific subdirectories
     with ‘i’ as needed
   • From the thumbnail buffer, ‘z’ (or the transient menu's Other
     group) inserts subdirectories recursively into the associated dired
     buffer; the ‘C-t s’ / ‘C-t z’ / ‘C-t k’ variants remain available
     from dired

14.2 Thumbnail Generation
=========================

First-time thumbnail generation can be slow for large images.
Thumbnails are cached by ‘image-dired’, so subsequent viewing is fast.

14.3 Display Quality and Speed
==============================

Lower display quality levels (‘faster’, ‘draft’) generate smaller
preview files using external tools, significantly reducing image decode
time.  For large photo collections, set the quality to ‘draft’ for the
fastest navigation, then switch to ‘full’ when you want to examine an
image in detail.


